the Curiosity rover is truly an  interplanetary Swiss Army knife equipped  with a slew of onboard instruments for  investigating the Red Planet the primary  mission was two years so we're into a  couple extended missions now yeah we  don't know exactly how long it'll keep  going we never know can't predict that  things are still going great on the  mission we're discovering new rock units  every day so we hope for several more  years to go  the rover has been slowly meandering  around Mars ever since it landed in 2012  using specialized instruments to examine  the surface perform experiments and even  find time to take a selfie these  instruments have led to some historic  discoveries in 2015  atmospheric greetings from the rover  suggested that Mars at one point  experienced a vigorous loss of  atmosphere causing its ancient oceans to  evaporate and last year curiosity  detected organic matter and sediment  samples taken from Gale Crater since  2012 the rover has been traversing the  154 km/h our senses want to know whether  Gale Crater was once completely filled  with sediment did it disappear over time  due to erosion or was the crater never  filled at all Gale Crater that the rover  is exploring in is five kilometers deep  Mount sharp is a five kilometre tall  mountain inside of that crater one of  the things that happens as you vary  sediments on the earth or any other  planet is that you increasingly compact  those sediments and lose the porosity  and those sediments will get denser and  denser and denser as you bury them to  greater depth the key to answering this  question lies in gravimetry the precise  measurement of gravitational fields  these fields are correlated with density  variations in the crust  unfortunately the rover wasn't equipped  with instruments designed to detect  these changes to get around this  researchers recalibrated curiosity's  accelerometers instruments normally used  for navigation to measure the changes in  gravitational acceleration as the rover  climbs the base of Mount sharp  you know I kind of realized one day that  gravity is just an acceleration and we  have accelerometers onboard and got kind  of curious about how good those  accelerometers were and and slowly began  to figure out calibration for that data  to the point where we had a precision  that was meaningful for understanding  the geology at the landing site after  five years of measurements scientists  were able to determine the density of  Gale Crater sedimentary rocks well in  this case one of the interesting things  is that the density was surprisingly low  even for sedimentary rocks which tend to  be lower density than say a lava right  the surprisingly low density tells us  that these sediments were likely never  buried to the full depth of Gale Crater  which would be five kilometers as the  mission rolls on researchers would like  to use curiosity's accelerometers for  another purpose to measure Mars as  seismic signals in hopes of detecting  Mars quake what we kind of show is that  they're not in great seismometers so we  would need either a pretty large  earthquake or a pretty nearby earthquake  to detect it at least in the  measurements that we're currently making  and while it's unlikely that curiosity  will detect a Mars quake the insight  Lander is designed to do just that  researchers would also like to solve the  mystery of how Mount sharp came to be  yes a mount sharp kind of continues to  be a puzzle we don't know especially for  the upper layers of the mountain how  that formed the whole thing is a stack  of layers and the layers are all tilted  outwards so it's almost like a haystack  in the middle of this five kilometer  deep crater so especially for those  upper units they may have kind of formed  as a free-standing haystack  rather than having filled the crater  entirely  but that's just a theory so we'll have  to get up there and figure it out  [Music]
 

